  {
   "id": "marvell-lumentum",
   "headline": "Marvell agrees to buy Lumentum for $115 billion in stock",
   "facts": [
    "All-stock 4.500 MRVL per LITE; EV ~$115B; ~$1,132/share; 23.9% premium to 28 Oct",
    "67/33 ownership; >$1B synergies; $550B TAM; close 1H 2028; $3.2B regulatory break fee",
    "LITE ~-18%, MRVL ~-13% on 2 Nov; IG analyst questions price"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0001633978/000119312526442758/d163169dex991.htm",
    "https://www.theglobeandmail.com/business/article-marvell-to-buy-lumentum-in-115-billion-deal-in-ai-data-centre-bet/",
    "https://www.cnbc.com/2026/11/01/stock-market-today-live-updates.html"
   ],
   "paragraph": "In an all-stock deal, the buyer pays with its own shares: each Lumentum share becomes 4.500 Marvell shares, like swapping one collectible card for four and a half of another. That implies about $115 billion including debt, roughly $1,132 a share, a 23.9% premium to the 28 October close. Afterwards Marvell holders own about 67% and Lumentum holders about 33%. The companies claim over $1 billion of yearly savings and a $550 billion combined market, pitched as an AI data-centre bet. Investors were unimpressed on Monday: Lumentum fell about 18%, Marvell about 13%. Is Marvell overpaying? An IG analyst asked that, and regulators must still approve before closing in the first half of 2028.",
   "edited": "none",
   "explainer": "sonnet"
  },
  {
   "id": "anthropic-s1a",
   "headline": "Anthropic's updated IPO filing shows $33 billion of revenue",
   "facts": [
    "Amended S-1 filed 2 Nov; 9M revenue $33.24B; Q3 ~$17B",
    "Net loss $104B mostly fair-value changes (single source)",
    "$895.4B commitments (single source); no price range"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "https://www.cnbc.com/2026/11/02/anthropic-ant-s-1-quarter-financials.html",
    "https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/live/stock-market-today-monday-november-2-dow-sp-500-nasdaq-091835095.html"
   ],
   "paragraph": "An S-1 is the filing a company makes with the SEC before listing shares, a first public look at its books. Anthropic's amended one shows revenue of $33.24 billion for the nine months to 30 September, about $17 billion of it in the third quarter. It also shows a net loss of $104 billion, which CNBC says comes mostly from accounting changes in the value of convertible notes and derivative liabilities rather than operations. Separately, about $895.4 billion of purchase commitments and leases are listed, substantially all for compute and infrastructure. Those last two figures rest on CNBC alone, no price range or ticker exists yet, and growth rates differ by outlet.",
   "edited": "none",
   "explainer": "sonnet"
  },
  {
   "id": "oracle-raise",
   "headline": "Oracle raises $20 billion in stock and bonds for AI",
   "facts": [
    "$10B stock + $10B bonds, six tranches 2029-2046, pricing not set",
    "Uses whole $20B cap through FY2030",
    "$125.34B debt vs $67.36B FY26 revenue; OpenAI exposure worry; stock -8.8% to $119.22"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "https://www.aol.com/articles/oracle-offer-10-billion-bonds-132211000.html",
    "https://www.fool.com/investing/2026/11/02/why-oracle-stock-is-down-today/"
   ],
   "paragraph": "Companies fund big projects by borrowing or by selling new slices of themselves. Oracle's filing does both: $10 billion of new common stock and $10 billion of bonds in six parts maturing 2029 to 2046, with pricing not yet set. That uses the whole $20 billion cap on extra AI financing through fiscal 2030 that Oracle set the week before. Selling new shares spreads ownership over more shares, so each existing share owns a smaller piece. Oracle had $125.34 billion of notes and borrowings at 31 August against fiscal 2026 revenue of $67.36 billion, and investors worried about its exposure to OpenAI. The stock fell 8.8% to $119.22.",
   "edited": "none",
   "explainer": "sonnet"
  },
  {
